lines=['Example 48 — conservative positivity and oscillation investigation','', 'Scope: fixed Manning n=0.020799605931143154, same measured widths, bed proxies and normal-flow outlet. Diagnostic copies only; no calibration or production changes. No collapses in this focused failure investigation.','', 'Conservative treatment: compute all proposed signed face fluxes; for each cell sum its outward fluxes, regardless of orientation. Scale all its outgoing faces by min(1, available_volume/(dt*sum_outward_flux)), with a tiny roundoff safety margin when active. Available volume is pre-step water plus positive external source; incoming internal flux is not spent in this step. Each face contributes the identical flux to donor and receiver. The limited flux also becomes momentum history. Deferred-transfer volume is an accumulated change in proposed transfer, NOT water lost.','', 'Focused tests passed: split outflows, reverse flows, outgoing boundary, incoming boundary, chained flow, external inflow, exact previously failing step, analytic uniform Manning equilibrium, inactive-limiter equivalence, lake at rest.','', 'EVENT RESULTS (all three conservative 50 m results remain hydraulically unacceptable):']
for x in results:
 a=x['audit'];lines.append(f"{x['tag']}: clipping {a['clipping_volume_m3']:.3g} m3; relative volume residual {x['relative_mass_residual']:.3g}; max depth {a['maximum_depth_m']:.6g} m; feature height {x['feature_height_cms']:.6g} m3/s; feature timing error {x['timing_error_minutes']:.1f} min; positivity-limited face-steps {a['positivity_limited_face_steps']:.0f}.")
lines+=['','100 m outlet hydrograph is bit-for-bit unchanged and the positivity limiter never activates. At 50 m, preventing water creation does not remove multi-metre depth excursions, large reversals and discharge spikes. The feature timing of these failed runs is not a valid calibration metric.','', 'ONSET: original 50 m run exceeds 1.2 m depth by hour 28.386 in reach 65; 1.5 m by hour 31.140 in reach 188; 2 m by hour 31.539 in reach 161; 3 m by hour 31.693 in reach 163. The first negative depth occurs later, hour 31.788 in reach 165. Therefore the first drying cell is not a unique source of the instability.','', 'CONTROLLED UNIFORM-CHANNEL TEST: periodic perturbations on a uniformly descending bed, constant width, no junctions, no physical outlet, no forcing change, no wet/dry front and no cap activation. Initial depth perturbation 1e-7 m, wavelength 4000 m, one-hour run. Normal-flow analogues use sampled depth, bed slope and n from physical reaches; they are NOT full-network replacements. A periodic seam includes the bed drop consistently. Across 27 combinations, observed growth agrees with the discrete linearized update within the required 0.1% tolerance (in fact much closer).','']
for reach in [65,161]:
 for dx in [100.,50.,25.]:
  z=modes[(modes.reach==reach)&(modes.dx==dx)];lines.append(f"Reach {reach} analogue, {dx:g} m: Fr={z.iloc[0].Fr:.4f}; amplitude factors {z.iloc[0].observed_amplification:.6f} (dt=3 s), {z.iloc[-1].observed_amplification:.6f} (dt=.75 s).")
lines+=['', 'Interpretation: the supplied update admits growing disturbances at representative hydraulics even without the network, outlet or clipping. Grid refinement reduces numerical damping; reach 161 analogue switches from decay at 100 m to growth at 50 m. Reducing timestep does not remove that spatial/model behaviour. This is strong mechanism evidence, not a unique decomposition of every full-network error. The 100 m match can mask instability and should not be treated as demonstrated convergence.','', 'LINEAR CHECK (derived here, not a universal validity threshold): for uniform positive flow, hflow=h_upstream and inactive cap, let r=g*dt*n^2*q/h^(7/3), A=(1-r)/(1+r), C=g*dt*((10/3)*S + h/dx*(1-exp(i*k)))/(1+r), D=dt/dx*(1-exp(-i*k)). The one-step [h,q] perturbation matrix is [[1-D*C,-D*A],[C,A]]. Initializing a tiny eigenmode gives measured amplitude |lambda|^steps. In the corresponding continuum uniform-flow linearization the subcharacteristic condition compares (5/3)u with sqrt(g*h); this is an idealized analytical diagnostic, not an acceptance rule for the real network.','', 'LITERATURE CONTEXT: draining-cell outgoing-flux limitation is established in finite-volume shallow-water methods; this implementation adapts that idea to our network, rather than reproducing an entire published solver: https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/AE2AC80D1E6E9F6BC0E68496A1C3EC52/S0962492918000028a.pdf/finitevolume_schemes_for_shallowwater_equations.pdf','de Almeida and Bates (2013) discuss applicability and numerical diffusion needed for some low-roughness runs: https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/wrcr.20366','de Almeida et al. (2012) specifically address improved numerical stability: https://eprints.soton.ac.uk/356385/2/WRR_2012.pdf','', 'RECOMMENDATION: retain this limiter as a diagnostic safeguard, not as a sufficient repair. Compare a documented stabilised local-inertial discretization against the original and a full shallow-water benchmark on the uniform-channel tests before promoting any revised network run. Stabilization must demonstrate grid/time convergence without tuning numerical smoothing merely to fit a gauge. Calibration should follow that verification.']
